00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.740
So to kick things
off, all of you

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:21.960
come from data
science backgrounds,

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.510
and I'm really curious
how that experience has

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:29.070
helped inform your ability to
manage a data science team.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:32.160
So I'll throw that
over to you first.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:32.870
Oh, sure.

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:37.170
So I my background
is in statistics,

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:40.050
that's what I went to
graduate school for

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:42.490
and that was a really
natural transition.

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:45.750
So one of my first jobs
out of grad school.

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:47.300
The title is senior analyst.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:51.090
I was working with health care
data and the responsibilities

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:55.200
and tasks, you know,
involved our skill working

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.590
with large databases, you
know, doing analyses, basically

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:02.760
all of the range of skills you
would expect a data scientist

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:03.930
to have today.

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:07.470
So you know, that
experience, of course,

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:09.910
was helpful to understand
the technical side.

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:12.210
But I think just
like any other job

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.400
where that's data
scientists or I

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.110
don't know marketing associate
anything the biggest and most

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:22.900
important part in terms
of getting to where I was

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:26.550
is learning how a business
functions and just

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:29.160
the day to day
operations, you know,

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:33.300
understanding the importance
of emotional intelligence,

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:37.590
working with other people, being
organized, paying attention

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:38.410
to process.

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:41.610
So those are, I think, more
important than the actual data

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:44.970
science technical
jobs themselves.

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:48.120
So it's just really
being open and curious

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:52.140
as most as all data scientists
are to those other aspects

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:52.750
of the job.

00:02:57.790 --> 00:02:58.800
Rhonda, how about you?

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.342
I'd love to hear how, you know,
coming from a data background

00:03:01.342 --> 00:03:04.510
has influenced your
management style.

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:08.440
Sure my management style
are the first three,

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:10.953
so I'm not
technically a manager.

00:03:10.953 --> 00:03:12.370
I'm an associate
technical fellow,

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:14.990
which is a slightly different
role than management.

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:18.310
We really take on the technical
responsibilities of the team

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:21.990
and guiding technical
vision for execution.

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:26.140
But I'll just kind of add to
my background is in statistics

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:29.200
and I have a Navy cat right now,
so I might just hold her here

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:32.110
because otherwise she'll
just keep jumping up here

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:35.980
and my I have an undergraduate
and a graduate degree

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:39.460
in anthropology, actually, which
is really different than most

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:41.290
people in this field.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:45.550
And what it especially like
my anthropology degree, what

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:47.860
happens is it just
makes me realize

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:49.812
the importance of
diversity on the team

00:03:49.812 --> 00:03:51.520
because people with
different backgrounds

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:53.770
and different skills, whether
you're from statistics,

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:57.520
US economics or, you know,
environmental science,

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:00.460
everybody brings a unique
perspective to solving

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:01.880
real problems, right?

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:03.260
And how we do that with data.

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:07.960
And so really, that's kind of
what I look for in a team is,

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:10.040
you know, do we have
different set of skills?

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:12.580
How can we bring those
skills and methodologies

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:13.990
to solve problems that we have?

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:21.800
Jarvis, how about you?

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:23.592
Can you tell us a bit
about your background

00:04:23.592 --> 00:04:27.560
and how that helps inform
managing a data science team?

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:29.180
Yeah, definitely.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:33.140
So I came up through the
team that I now manage,

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:35.060
so I started as an
analyst on that team

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:38.140
and sort of worked my way up
to be in charge of it now.

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:40.550
And I think the biggest
thing is that when you've

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:43.790
done the work yourself, it
gives you a very, I think,

00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:48.450
good idea of how to ask
people to do that work.

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:51.440
And I try to be sort
of like the boss.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.390
Like, I think it's easy
for me to ask myself,

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:56.010
like, what was my ideal
boss when I was an analyst?

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:57.878
And then how do I
try to become that?

00:04:57.878 --> 00:04:59.420
And I think it works
well when you've

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:03.112
done the work, because that
vision tends to be more aligned

00:05:03.112 --> 00:05:05.570
with something that's achievable
and good for the business.

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:07.670
But when you're
managing people and you

00:05:07.670 --> 00:05:09.320
don't share those
experiences, it's

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:12.590
a lot harder to one formulate
what that good boss looks like

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:14.400
and then also to become it.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:17.182
And in some cases, it may be
almost unreasonable, right?

00:05:17.182 --> 00:05:19.640
If you're in charge of a bunch
of different groups of teams

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:21.212
that sort of
competing priorities,

00:05:21.212 --> 00:05:22.670
you may need to
make decisions that

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:24.130
benefit one over the other.

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:26.215
But having done the
work and then having

00:05:26.215 --> 00:05:27.590
been in charge of
the small team,

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:30.260
I think I have a very good
idea of what it entails

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:32.660
and also how to make sure
that it's communicated well

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:34.130
and it maximizes impact.

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:36.740
So sort of being
the person I wanted

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:39.260
to have when I was doing
it as how I go about it,

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:41.420
I think it's worked
pretty well so far,

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:42.980
but that's sort of my strategy.

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:50.160
Yeah cool.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:52.650
And then I guess, yeah,
from my perspective,

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:55.680
I would kind of echo
everything everyone just said.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:59.010
I'd say some ads
there would be coming

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:01.183
from a PhD program or academia.

00:06:01.183 --> 00:06:02.850
I think I'm more
sensitive on the hiring

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:05.550
front to the skill
set that they bring,

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:10.710
even though oftentimes they will
lack an actual job experience

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:12.570
outside of academia.

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:16.260
Just thinking through their
experience as job experience

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:19.050
is really important, and I
think that can be valuable skill

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:20.245
sets to the team.

00:06:20.245 --> 00:06:21.870
But then also knowing
kind of the skill

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:23.328
gaps that they'll
come with, right,

00:06:23.328 --> 00:06:26.850
since they have not worked in
more of an industry setting.

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:29.430
And then I think one thing I
didn't appreciate in my old job

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.030
that I do appreciate
in my new job.

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:33.120
So my old job, I was
a data scientist.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:34.560
There was no really
analytics team

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.100
was more of a consulting firm.

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:38.740
My new job, I started out
managing the analytics team.

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:42.060
So I think finally, I've
gotten kind of a full circle

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:44.370
lens of how those two
teams can really complement

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:47.220
one another, data science and
analytics, the differences

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:51.060
and kind of the complementary
nature between the two.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:54.060
Yeah so I'd say that
definitely informs

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:57.690
my hiring of data
scientists knowing, hey,

00:06:57.690 --> 00:06:59.983
where do we want to take
and pick up some of the work

00:06:59.983 --> 00:07:01.150
that the analysts have done?

00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:05.687
And how will those two teams
and to folks work together so?

00:07:05.687 --> 00:07:06.770
Yeah, that's really great.

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:08.870
You've kind of queued up
for this next question,

00:07:08.870 --> 00:07:10.520
so I'm going to start
with you, Katie.

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:12.200
But I'd really love to hear.

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:15.917
I know that everyone watching
has questions about this,

00:07:15.917 --> 00:07:17.750
and I know we all have
experience with this,

00:07:17.750 --> 00:07:20.150
but certainly with
Katie, how do you feel

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:23.870
or what do you feel are the best
ways to assess a candidate's

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:26.350
technical ability?

00:07:26.350 --> 00:07:30.880
Yeah, so a lot of different
options for this, of course.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:35.710
I think my goal is a hiring
manager is just again

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:37.430
to your first question, right?

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:40.630
Having done the work yourself
to at least some degree,

00:07:40.630 --> 00:07:43.088
I think, allows you to just
assess in some of their answers

00:07:43.088 --> 00:07:44.713
do they know what
they're talking about

00:07:44.713 --> 00:07:47.350
or is there kind of a level of
are they reaching a little bit

00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:49.887
too far on some of the skills?

00:07:49.887 --> 00:07:52.220
So I think that my technical
experience helps with that.

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:55.240
But I think one thing
we do here at beam

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:58.290
is we have a take home
test, a simulated data.

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:00.855
I think it is really
important to assess

00:08:00.855 --> 00:08:02.480
those technical skills
in an interview.

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:04.730
But I think the one thing
that I'm really not a fan of

00:08:04.730 --> 00:08:07.090
is kind of to a
point, I think certain

00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:10.360
more traditional like
whiteboarding or brainteaser

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:11.458
questions and interviews.

00:08:11.458 --> 00:08:13.000
I think at a certain
point, they kind

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:15.100
of just measure
performance under duress

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:17.350
versus performance.

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:20.110
So I think there's a happy
medium between whether you're

00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:24.100
doing kind of a live technical
interview, live coding,

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:29.080
making that productive and worth
the time in terms of assessing

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:30.940
the skills you want to assess.

00:08:30.940 --> 00:08:34.120
Take home is certainly another
route that I become a fan of,

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:37.090
but I've seen the kind of more
live technical interviews done

00:08:37.090 --> 00:08:40.563
well and then some maybe not
so well where folks might have

00:08:40.563 --> 00:08:41.980
missed out on some
good candidates

00:08:41.980 --> 00:08:44.710
just because they asked a brain
teaser that the person didn't

00:08:44.710 --> 00:08:47.200
get in those five minutes.

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:49.020
So that's kind of my quick take.

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:54.720
And I'll kind of follow up.

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:58.238
I say I'm a huge fan
of them basically

00:08:58.238 --> 00:09:00.030
talking about what
they're passionate about

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:03.390
and describing situations where
they've actually applied it

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:07.950
and some of the key criteria
you know that we probably

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:10.550
look for in some of those
answers are like, you know,

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:13.290
somebody coming up to you and
asking you for advice on how

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:15.750
to solve these problems, right?

00:09:15.750 --> 00:09:18.550
Are you being a mentor
to somebody else?

00:09:18.550 --> 00:09:20.798
Because that's usually
a way that you can share

00:09:20.798 --> 00:09:22.090
your skills with somebody else.

00:09:22.090 --> 00:09:23.507
That means you're
confident enough

00:09:23.507 --> 00:09:25.500
to demonstrate your
skills and then share

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:29.430
that with somebody else and
and basically teach, right?

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:33.090
Also, your know, your
ability to describe it

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:35.220
in a technical manner and
a situation in which you

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:35.860
applied it.

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:37.890
Because honestly,
like, you know,

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:40.110
people come from a variety
of different backgrounds,

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:41.970
there's a whole bunch
of different language

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:45.220
that different backgrounds use
to describe the same processes.

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:48.510
And so, you know, I
really like the approach

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:51.940
of asking them to describe a
situation that they've been in.

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:55.140
What was the outcome of
that and really describe

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:58.770
the result in a technical
format in you can usually

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:00.990
tell when somebody is
passionate about something

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:05.160
and let them kind of pursue
their passions a little bit,

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:07.500
but at the same time,
making sure that the skills

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:08.980
that we need for the job, right?

00:10:12.590 --> 00:10:16.730
I agree a technical exercise
is really important.

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:18.500
I've always used
them, it's, you know,

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:23.960
it's really the only way you can
quickly and accurately assess

00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:26.570
the technical skills.

00:10:26.570 --> 00:10:30.740
So I think it's
required also just,

00:10:30.740 --> 00:10:33.440
you know, the basic
integrating and job

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:36.680
seeking 101
expectations, you know?

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:38.640
Well, put together resume.

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:41.250
Of course, no typos,
things like that.

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:45.470
Things they teach you
over and over again.

00:10:45.470 --> 00:10:48.890
And then over time,
at least, I like

00:10:48.890 --> 00:10:52.110
to think I've developed
like an intuition also.

00:10:52.110 --> 00:10:54.360
You know, it's well,
it's more than intuition.

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:55.818
It's, you know,
it's on the resume.

00:10:55.818 --> 00:10:58.410
It's also in the actual
interview process themselves.

00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:01.730
You can start to
assess to the extent

00:11:01.730 --> 00:11:05.960
that person's really curious
and passionate about the work

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:07.550
itself.

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:11.840
So curiosity is pure
curiosity and energy

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:16.160
to me are my number, number one
and two traits that I look for.

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:20.370
You know, if you've only
learned how to write are on the,

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:24.980
you know, for a couple of
years or in grad school,

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:26.750
I think you can pick
those things up,

00:11:26.750 --> 00:11:28.970
but you can't you
can't teach someone

00:11:28.970 --> 00:11:34.040
to be really curious and
really energetic, you know?

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:37.190
Yeah and then
finally, when we do

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:39.310
start having
technical discussions,

00:11:39.310 --> 00:11:42.440
I think this is reflected
in what Rhonda said.

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:45.195
You know, it's a matter
of does this person know

00:11:45.195 --> 00:11:46.320
what they're talking about?

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:48.770
And so I listen for,
are they being specific

00:11:48.770 --> 00:11:50.510
in what they're describing?

00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:54.230
If they start speaking in
generalities and, you know,

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:57.020
vague answers, you
know, pretty quickly,

00:11:57.020 --> 00:11:59.450
they don't really they
aren't really well suited

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:00.480
for this kind of job.

00:12:03.460 --> 00:12:04.340
For sure, Yeah.

00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:06.490
I'm an echo, a lot of
things that were said,

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:08.590
I'm on team take
home as well, and I

00:12:08.590 --> 00:12:11.080
hate doing brain
teasers and interviews

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:13.040
or even asking
people to do them.

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:16.540
It's I don't know, it feels
like torture a lot of the time.

00:12:16.540 --> 00:12:19.090
But a plea for hiring
managers out there, you know,

00:12:19.090 --> 00:12:20.710
try to make sure
that you're asking

00:12:20.710 --> 00:12:22.480
sort of as little
of folks that you

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:24.100
can to get a good
assessment on them

00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:25.990
to just be respectful
of their time.

00:12:25.990 --> 00:12:28.660
Because what happens is and I
think it works in your favor

00:12:28.660 --> 00:12:30.868
because you have some very
bright folks out there who

00:12:30.868 --> 00:12:32.410
are like, hey, you
know, I don't know

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:34.060
if I need to invest
$4 hours into this

00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:35.253
when I have two offers.

00:12:35.253 --> 00:12:36.670
So you're going
to turn away folks

00:12:36.670 --> 00:12:39.910
who really do know what they're
doing if you make it to sort

00:12:39.910 --> 00:12:43.252
of heavy and the opposite
logic, which I don't agree

00:12:43.252 --> 00:12:44.710
with as well, if
they really cared,

00:12:44.710 --> 00:12:46.502
they would sink in all
the time and energy,

00:12:46.502 --> 00:12:48.178
and I just don't
agree with that.

00:12:48.178 --> 00:12:49.720
But for us, you
know, I think there's

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:51.760
a big concern that,
well, what if they get

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:53.140
a lot of help on the take home?

00:12:53.140 --> 00:12:54.920
You're not really
assessing their ability.

00:12:54.920 --> 00:12:57.250
So we like to ask a lot
of follow up questions

00:12:57.250 --> 00:12:59.020
during the interviews,
and we'll usually

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:00.670
use one of our
interviews to say,

00:13:00.670 --> 00:13:03.070
OK, you made this decision
here, you made this choice

00:13:03.070 --> 00:13:04.215
or using this technique.

00:13:04.215 --> 00:13:05.840
Can you tell me a
little more about it?

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:08.138
And then we're doing what
you said of, you know,

00:13:08.138 --> 00:13:10.180
is this something that
your friend told you to do

00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:12.347
and you have no idea how
the technique works and you

00:13:12.347 --> 00:13:13.340
can't answer questions?

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:15.280
Or is it something that
you have knowledge of?

00:13:15.280 --> 00:13:17.680
So that's how we sort of try
to bring that all together

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:20.380
in our interview process.

00:13:20.380 --> 00:13:21.850
Now, that's really interesting.

00:13:21.850 --> 00:13:23.740
A lot of great, great
perspectives here,

00:13:23.740 --> 00:13:26.380
and we've had a question
from the audience that really

00:13:26.380 --> 00:13:28.000
dovetails nicely with this.

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:31.150
So I'd be really curious
if you change the way

00:13:31.150 --> 00:13:32.830
that you assess
a candidate based

00:13:32.830 --> 00:13:35.690
on their kind of
educational background.

00:13:35.690 --> 00:13:38.140
So, for example,
data science degrees

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:40.420
are now a thing
that people can get.

00:13:40.420 --> 00:13:42.670
But if someone comes from
a quantitative background,

00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:45.370
computer science
statistics, data science

00:13:45.370 --> 00:13:50.230
versus someone coming from just
a non quantitative background,

00:13:50.230 --> 00:13:54.010
education or policy
or literature,

00:13:54.010 --> 00:13:58.690
do you change how you assess
that individual in an interview

00:13:58.690 --> 00:14:01.750
or no, maybe everybody's a
no, but I'd be really curious

00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:04.240
if there's anything
how you kind of May

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:05.910
approach that
differently, if at all.

00:14:09.787 --> 00:14:11.870
I'd say that the first
thing that sticks out to me

00:14:11.870 --> 00:14:13.580
is a little bit
less of what I do,

00:14:13.580 --> 00:14:15.122
kind of where the
conversation heads,

00:14:15.122 --> 00:14:17.738
but to me, it's really important
you, whatever your applicant

00:14:17.738 --> 00:14:20.030
management system is, it's
really important that you're

00:14:20.030 --> 00:14:23.330
keeping up with the recruiting,
like the lead recruiter on it

00:14:23.330 --> 00:14:25.700
to make sure that person
gets an interview.

00:14:25.700 --> 00:14:28.430
That's what I found to be the
most crucial way in which I

00:14:28.430 --> 00:14:30.800
step in which I typically
wouldn't is just

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:34.070
giving the head recruiter that
like commenting when we see

00:14:34.070 --> 00:14:37.375
the application and seeing
stupor on top of it myself,

00:14:37.375 --> 00:14:39.500
because it's a little bit
harder sometimes for them

00:14:39.500 --> 00:14:41.030
to spot like, oh,
well, this person

00:14:41.030 --> 00:14:42.680
with this nontraditional
background

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:45.380
has the skills you want or not.

00:14:45.380 --> 00:14:48.470
So I just make sure that I get
a chance to talk to that person

00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:51.770
if I see that they've got
some components that are maybe

00:14:51.770 --> 00:14:56.210
nontraditional, but
speak to their skill set.

00:14:56.210 --> 00:14:58.360
And I'll kind of
add that as somebody

00:14:58.360 --> 00:15:00.820
who comes from a more
nontraditional state

00:15:00.820 --> 00:15:01.540
background.

00:15:01.540 --> 00:15:04.010
I graduated with a
liberal arts degree.

00:15:04.010 --> 00:15:05.680
And then I got my start degree.

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:10.840
But I will say like personally,
as I entered job interviewing,

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:13.000
I was worried that my
anthropology degree

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:15.045
would actually set me back.

00:15:15.045 --> 00:15:16.420
But the reality,
I mean, it still

00:15:16.420 --> 00:15:19.160
had to start like, I still
have a master's in stats.

00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:23.800
But the reality is it brought an
element that differentiated me

00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:24.890
from the candidates.

00:15:24.890 --> 00:15:30.360
And I will say at Boeing,
everybody gets the same.

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:33.090
Opportunity of interview for
that, that so everybody gets

00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:35.130
the same question, so
as far as that goes,

00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:38.010
that that's a standardized
process that our HR has

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:40.140
developed, right?

00:15:40.140 --> 00:15:43.200
But I would encourage
like any people that

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:45.570
just have a solid like
science background

00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:49.080
to go out and discover the
liberal arts because it

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:50.340
can only make you better.

00:15:50.340 --> 00:15:52.140
Right?

00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:55.475
that's my personal view,
and I love that, right?

00:15:55.475 --> 00:15:57.600
So if you're coming from
a quantitative background,

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:00.030
go out, discover
do things outside

00:16:00.030 --> 00:16:02.570
of your educational background.

00:16:02.570 --> 00:16:04.178
So this really ties
into a question,

00:16:04.178 --> 00:16:05.470
and I'd love to start with you.

00:16:05.470 --> 00:16:08.010
Jerry's skills like
empathy and communication

00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:10.290
are often mentioned
is like absolute

00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:12.680
must haves for data scientists.

00:16:12.680 --> 00:16:17.630
But how do a candidate
has these skills?

00:16:17.630 --> 00:16:21.322
Yeah, I went through this great
training at work a while ago,

00:16:21.322 --> 00:16:23.030
and it was sort of a
generalist training,

00:16:23.030 --> 00:16:24.770
it was not for hiring
technical roles,

00:16:24.770 --> 00:16:27.290
it was just hiring in
general, and they went over

00:16:27.290 --> 00:16:30.210
a really good framework for
asking behavioral questions.

00:16:30.210 --> 00:16:32.030
And what I thought
was so unusual

00:16:32.030 --> 00:16:34.760
about it was that, like
the questions to me,

00:16:34.760 --> 00:16:36.480
seemed to have obvious answers.

00:16:36.480 --> 00:16:39.350
But if you ask 10 people
or 10 data scientists,

00:16:39.350 --> 00:16:41.120
you're going to get
rid of three of them

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:42.662
because they're not
going to give you

00:16:42.662 --> 00:16:44.190
the answer that you would want.

00:16:44.190 --> 00:16:47.420
And so what we do
is we ask these,

00:16:47.420 --> 00:16:49.887
we put them in situations
that are sort of undesirable.

00:16:49.887 --> 00:16:51.720
And I don't mean literally
in the interview.

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:53.450
I mean, we asked
them about situations

00:16:53.450 --> 00:16:54.870
that are undesirable.

00:16:54.870 --> 00:16:57.800
You know, you've gotten a vague
request from a stakeholder

00:16:57.800 --> 00:17:00.210
and we see how they deal
with those situations.

00:17:00.210 --> 00:17:03.710
And I think folks who deal
well with that first say,

00:17:03.710 --> 00:17:06.589
OK, you know, I get that
this happens on the job

00:17:06.589 --> 00:17:08.690
and I will work with them
to come to an answer.

00:17:08.690 --> 00:17:12.260
But there's always a subset of
folks who kind of put it back

00:17:12.260 --> 00:17:14.480
on you and is like, well,
isn't your job to prevent

00:17:14.480 --> 00:17:15.720
these things from happening?

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:17.690
Or like, why would I
be in this position?

00:17:17.690 --> 00:17:19.506
And it's, you know,
as much as I'd

00:17:19.506 --> 00:17:21.589
love to live in a world
in which it never happens,

00:17:21.589 --> 00:17:22.819
it always happens.

00:17:22.819 --> 00:17:24.810
And so it's kind of an easy way.

00:17:24.810 --> 00:17:26.660
I found to ask those
behavioral questions

00:17:26.660 --> 00:17:29.810
about difficult situations that
involve working with people

00:17:29.810 --> 00:17:31.970
or communicating
things to people

00:17:31.970 --> 00:17:35.150
and seeing sort of how flexible
data scientists applicants can

00:17:35.150 --> 00:17:37.365
be in their answers.

00:17:37.365 --> 00:17:39.740
So it was funny to me because
when I first learned these,

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:42.080
I'm like, well, no one's
going to say like, I shouldn't

00:17:42.080 --> 00:17:44.000
do that or that's not
my job, but you end up

00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:46.862
picking up a few people who
genuinely do feel that way.

00:17:46.862 --> 00:17:48.320
And and, you know,
I'm sure they're

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:50.010
a great fit for
another role out there.

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:51.850
But but that role
isn't one of is.

00:17:58.060 --> 00:17:59.890
Yeah, for sure, it's
and it's kind of

00:17:59.890 --> 00:18:02.960
nice to get to that
point where, you know,

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:06.070
like you don't want to
pull a candidate along

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:08.060
if they're not a good fit.

00:18:08.060 --> 00:18:10.010
So as we're talking
about hiring,

00:18:10.010 --> 00:18:13.060
I I'd love to hear from
you to kick things off

00:18:13.060 --> 00:18:16.060
in terms of how do you know
when it's time to grow your data

00:18:16.060 --> 00:18:18.340
science team and kind
of to piggyback off

00:18:18.340 --> 00:18:21.820
of that, what strategies do
you employ when you're growing

00:18:21.820 --> 00:18:24.130
your team?

00:18:24.130 --> 00:18:27.790
Of everywhere I've been.

00:18:27.790 --> 00:18:29.740
It's always, well,
I've always been

00:18:29.740 --> 00:18:31.900
hired because they
needed to grow the team,

00:18:31.900 --> 00:18:35.110
so they hired me
to grow the team.

00:18:35.110 --> 00:18:39.610
So it's the answer is
always pretty much most,

00:18:39.610 --> 00:18:41.980
you know, for most
firms that are

00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:47.030
data rich obviously could stand
to use more data scientists.

00:18:47.030 --> 00:18:51.880
But, you know, to answer
your question directly,

00:18:51.880 --> 00:18:55.530
it's really a matter
of demand, right?

00:18:55.530 --> 00:18:57.850
Less I've been doing
this for a long time,

00:18:57.850 --> 00:19:01.630
and we just never have enough
data scientists, the type

00:19:01.630 --> 00:19:03.340
of teams that I've managed.

00:19:03.340 --> 00:19:06.910
I've always been internal
support and external support,

00:19:06.910 --> 00:19:09.010
supporting clients and
supporting other functions

00:19:09.010 --> 00:19:13.040
like marketing, sales,
operations, et cetera.

00:19:13.040 --> 00:19:18.160
So the appetite for data
is, you know, bottomless.

00:19:18.160 --> 00:19:22.210
So we're always in growth mode.

00:19:22.210 --> 00:19:27.040
And then when it comes to
actually building out the team,

00:19:27.040 --> 00:19:29.680
which I'm currently in
the situation, you know,

00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:32.170
I really want to
try to build out

00:19:32.170 --> 00:19:37.150
a team that's diverse in all
aspects, you know, technically

00:19:37.150 --> 00:19:39.490
and experience wise.

00:19:39.490 --> 00:19:42.490
You know, there is no such
thing as a data scientist

00:19:42.490 --> 00:19:44.290
who can do everything.

00:19:44.290 --> 00:19:46.900
I also don't want to have a data
scientist where everyone has

00:19:46.900 --> 00:19:48.450
the exact same set of skills.

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:52.258
So you may have people who are
more experienced with the data

00:19:52.258 --> 00:19:53.800
engineering side,
people who are more

00:19:53.800 --> 00:19:56.210
experienced on the statistical
modeling side, et cetera.

00:19:56.210 --> 00:19:59.410
In terms of experience,
you know, having people

00:19:59.410 --> 00:20:03.250
from different backgrounds
in terms of degrees, that's

00:20:03.250 --> 00:20:05.740
a great thing because they
bring different skill sets

00:20:05.740 --> 00:20:09.790
and people from different
industries, you know,

00:20:09.790 --> 00:20:14.430
because that allows, you know?

00:20:14.430 --> 00:20:16.238
Everyone brings
their best practices,

00:20:16.238 --> 00:20:18.030
and we can learn, at
least from each other,

00:20:18.030 --> 00:20:19.650
learn and grow from each other.

00:20:19.650 --> 00:20:22.830
And that's how I've done
that, that's how I've done it,

00:20:22.830 --> 00:20:24.280
and it's worked well so far.

00:20:27.068 --> 00:20:28.860
I really love that
statement that everybody

00:20:28.860 --> 00:20:31.140
brings their best
practices, I think

00:20:31.140 --> 00:20:33.870
that that's just
such a wonderful way

00:20:33.870 --> 00:20:36.420
to think about growing a team.

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:39.450
So I would also really
love to hear from folks.

00:20:39.450 --> 00:20:42.480
We have a question from the
audience around managers

00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:44.010
staying in the code.

00:20:44.010 --> 00:20:46.470
So in other words, what are
your thoughts and feelings

00:20:46.470 --> 00:20:48.670
about management
and being a manager?

00:20:48.670 --> 00:20:51.900
And should a manager still be
able to rate and review code

00:20:51.900 --> 00:20:54.400
as well as solve
technical questions?

00:20:59.300 --> 00:21:00.360
If you're at a startup.

00:21:00.360 --> 00:21:06.860
Yes yeah, I've flamed out
a few times, a few startups

00:21:06.860 --> 00:21:10.250
and you know, not surprisingly,
your short staffed,

00:21:10.250 --> 00:21:13.310
so you're expected to pitch in.

00:21:13.310 --> 00:21:15.650
But in my experience, this
is just my experience only

00:21:15.650 --> 00:21:17.930
in health care.

00:21:17.930 --> 00:21:21.440
You know, once you go
beyond the director level,

00:21:21.440 --> 00:21:23.240
I think it's important
for the manager

00:21:23.240 --> 00:21:28.800
to actually be the one
while managing because.

00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:32.430
Uh, again, this goes
back to my firm belief

00:21:32.430 --> 00:21:34.260
that process is
really important,

00:21:34.260 --> 00:21:36.150
you know, you can't
just have people just

00:21:36.150 --> 00:21:38.790
running around doing
whatever they want to do,

00:21:38.790 --> 00:21:40.030
you have to steer the ship.

00:21:40.030 --> 00:21:45.260
Everyone in the right direction,
it's a lot of work, you know?

00:21:45.260 --> 00:21:48.680
Get, I can still read our,
you know, a little bit,

00:21:48.680 --> 00:21:53.030
but, you know, I won't be nearly
as efficient as the people who

00:21:53.030 --> 00:21:55.220
actually much
better at it, so you

00:21:55.220 --> 00:21:57.890
don't want me writing the code.

00:21:57.890 --> 00:22:00.500
Instead, you want
me going out there.

00:22:00.500 --> 00:22:03.320
You want your manager
going out there in

00:22:03.320 --> 00:22:06.080
and think of it this way,
finding more business

00:22:06.080 --> 00:22:06.868
for the team.

00:22:06.868 --> 00:22:08.660
Because finding more
business for the team,

00:22:08.660 --> 00:22:10.190
whether it's
internal or external,

00:22:10.190 --> 00:22:13.860
means more opportunities
for you to do other things.

00:22:13.860 --> 00:22:17.060
You know, working in
other subject areas

00:22:17.060 --> 00:22:19.910
add additional technical skills.

00:22:19.910 --> 00:22:24.960
So that's how I think
that's how I view myself.

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:27.902
I'll kind of add, because
I'm not a manager, right?

00:22:27.902 --> 00:22:30.360
But that's one of the great
and awesome things about Boeing

00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:32.700
is we kind of have
different career paths

00:22:32.700 --> 00:22:35.130
so you can go into
management or you

00:22:35.130 --> 00:22:36.970
can be just a really
awesome employee,

00:22:36.970 --> 00:22:39.300
or you can go into the
technical fellowship, which

00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:42.940
is a career path driven
towards technical excellence.

00:22:42.940 --> 00:22:46.622
And like, like I'm an air
Boeing designated expert, right?

00:22:46.622 --> 00:22:48.830
There's not very many of
those at the Boeing company.

00:22:48.830 --> 00:22:50.200
And so that's awesome.

00:22:50.200 --> 00:22:52.860
Like I can help you
program and management

00:22:52.860 --> 00:22:55.380
can really focus
on those more air

00:22:55.380 --> 00:22:58.470
and going out and getting
the business things

00:22:58.470 --> 00:23:00.720
while at the same time,
technical Fellows also

00:23:00.720 --> 00:23:03.030
can kind of participate in
that, going out and getting

00:23:03.030 --> 00:23:06.150
the business and where the
technical vision and things

00:23:06.150 --> 00:23:06.920
like that are.

00:23:06.920 --> 00:23:10.140
And so I think I
like Boeing for that

00:23:10.140 --> 00:23:13.350
because if you really want to
focus on technical excellence,

00:23:13.350 --> 00:23:16.380
you can really drive your
career in that direction

00:23:16.380 --> 00:23:20.790
and you don't necessarily
have to end up in management.

00:23:20.790 --> 00:23:21.550
It's all in.

00:23:25.240 --> 00:23:26.040
Yeah, that's great.

00:23:26.040 --> 00:23:27.790
And with that being
said, though, managers

00:23:27.790 --> 00:23:29.840
should still be data literate.

00:23:29.840 --> 00:23:30.340
That's a.

00:23:32.990 --> 00:23:35.150
I love it, I love the
enthusiasm Katie and Jerry

00:23:35.150 --> 00:23:37.160
has anything to add on that.

00:23:37.160 --> 00:23:39.620
A lot of nodding, so I'm not
sure for all like, yeah, this,

00:23:39.620 --> 00:23:40.470
I agree with this.

00:23:40.470 --> 00:23:42.450
Or maybe someone is
like, no, absolutely.

00:23:42.450 --> 00:23:45.710
Managers should be
coding every day.

00:23:45.710 --> 00:23:48.600
I think it really, just
like others have said,

00:23:48.600 --> 00:23:50.270
depends on the stage
of the company.

00:23:50.270 --> 00:23:53.480
So I think at a certain point
it while it sounds nice,

00:23:53.480 --> 00:23:54.510
I think it'd be fun.

00:23:54.510 --> 00:23:58.100
It can be unrealistic to
expect that somebody who's

00:23:58.100 --> 00:23:59.930
managing multiple
different teams or folks

00:23:59.930 --> 00:24:03.360
has the time to program
considerably every day.

00:24:03.360 --> 00:24:05.840
But I do think you need to
have a level of competency

00:24:05.840 --> 00:24:09.920
and ideally you have had
experience in the role

00:24:09.920 --> 00:24:12.350
or akin to the role
in your history

00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:15.560
because I think if you don't
have enough experience,

00:24:15.560 --> 00:24:17.750
it can be hard to
spot the root cause

00:24:17.750 --> 00:24:20.060
of some like personnel issues
or maybe other friction

00:24:20.060 --> 00:24:20.910
you're experiencing.

00:24:20.910 --> 00:24:22.910
Because if you can't go
in and assess the issues

00:24:22.910 --> 00:24:25.263
with the code or
the performance,

00:24:25.263 --> 00:24:27.180
it'll be hard for you
as a manager to do that.

00:24:27.180 --> 00:24:29.210
But at a certain
point, hopefully you've

00:24:29.210 --> 00:24:32.360
staffed up a successor who
is able to do that for you,

00:24:32.360 --> 00:24:35.612
so you can focus on the other
kind of higher level things

00:24:35.612 --> 00:24:37.320
in terms of where are
we taking the team?

00:24:37.320 --> 00:24:39.560
What kind of projects
are we taking on?

00:24:39.560 --> 00:24:43.700
But for me, it is important
that I do write code from time

00:24:43.700 --> 00:24:46.970
to time, especially as
projects are kicking off.

00:24:46.970 --> 00:24:48.350
But certainly, I
think it depends

00:24:48.350 --> 00:24:49.850
on where you're at in terms
of whether that should

00:24:49.850 --> 00:24:51.770
be a daily thing or
you should be actually

00:24:51.770 --> 00:24:53.840
moving projects
forward yourself.

00:24:53.840 --> 00:24:55.700
But I think having
that skill set is

00:24:55.700 --> 00:24:59.210
important to make sure your
team is functioning properly

00:24:59.210 --> 00:25:01.340
and you're hiring
the right people.

00:25:01.340 --> 00:25:02.130
Yeah, absolutely.

00:25:02.130 --> 00:25:03.920
And I love this
emphasis on, you know,

00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:06.612
you've mentioned where if
you're starting a project,

00:25:06.612 --> 00:25:08.070
you're much more
involved with code

00:25:08.070 --> 00:25:12.110
and you mentioned a lot about
the stage of the organization.

00:25:12.110 --> 00:25:13.962
So I kind of want to
pivot a little bit

00:25:13.962 --> 00:25:15.420
and I'll throw it
to Jerry's first.

00:25:15.420 --> 00:25:16.837
But I want to talk
about something

00:25:16.837 --> 00:25:19.850
that, at least on social media,
I think it's a lot of play

00:25:19.850 --> 00:25:23.330
and that is about hiring
someone with the potential

00:25:23.330 --> 00:25:24.370
to learn and grow.

00:25:24.370 --> 00:25:27.350
So we see a lot of
advice from things

00:25:27.350 --> 00:25:31.942
like I don't need 100% of the
requirements or I only meet 60%

00:25:31.942 --> 00:25:33.650
And so you'll see next
advice from things

00:25:33.650 --> 00:25:36.067
like, oh, as long as you meet
50 percent, you should apply

00:25:36.067 --> 00:25:38.047
or it doesn't even
matter, just apply for it.

00:25:38.047 --> 00:25:38.880
Like just go for it.

00:25:38.880 --> 00:25:40.588
And then see people
were like, no, you've

00:25:40.588 --> 00:25:42.800
got to be able to
do 80% or 90% So

00:25:42.800 --> 00:25:46.580
when we're thinking about hiring
someone with the potential

00:25:46.580 --> 00:25:48.180
to learn and grow.

00:25:48.180 --> 00:25:52.460
How do you assess
this in a candidate?

00:25:52.460 --> 00:25:54.500
Yeah, that's a great
that's a great question,

00:25:54.500 --> 00:25:57.260
I think almost everybody is
in this boat where they're not

00:25:57.260 --> 00:26:00.990
matching all of the criteria
that have been laid out.

00:26:00.990 --> 00:26:04.910
So if the company does,
indeed box number seven

00:26:04.910 --> 00:26:06.120
does indeed need that.

00:26:06.120 --> 00:26:08.370
Like, you have to convince
them that this is something

00:26:08.370 --> 00:26:09.860
I can pick up over time.

00:26:09.860 --> 00:26:11.690
I think one of the
best indicators

00:26:11.690 --> 00:26:14.400
is just a track record of
having done this in the past.

00:26:14.400 --> 00:26:16.490
So when you're positioning
yourself for the role,

00:26:16.490 --> 00:26:18.980
if you can look at prior
experience and say,

00:26:18.980 --> 00:26:21.050
I did this role and
I didn't have xyz,

00:26:21.050 --> 00:26:23.540
but these are things that I
picked up in the context of it.

00:26:23.540 --> 00:26:25.010
I think that's a really
strong, you know,

00:26:25.010 --> 00:26:26.718
you're showing that
I've done this before

00:26:26.718 --> 00:26:28.508
and I'm looking to do it again.

00:26:28.508 --> 00:26:30.800
So that's the best way I've
seen a position because you

00:26:30.800 --> 00:26:32.840
do have a lot of candidates
who say, yeah, I'll pick it up,

00:26:32.840 --> 00:26:34.910
but only a subset of
them are like, here's

00:26:34.910 --> 00:26:37.250
sort of my track record
of having done this

00:26:37.250 --> 00:26:40.932
in the past and prior roles
or in school or what have you.

00:26:40.932 --> 00:26:42.890
Yeah, I think actually
school is a good example

00:26:42.890 --> 00:26:45.710
as well because you have folks
who need to learn a programming

00:26:45.710 --> 00:26:47.810
language to do a
specific project

00:26:47.810 --> 00:26:50.420
and so they can bring
that to light as well.

00:26:50.420 --> 00:26:52.850
But I think showing that track
record is really effective

00:26:52.850 --> 00:26:55.590
because it goes beyond saying,
you know, trust me, I'll do it.

00:26:55.590 --> 00:26:58.048
But like, trust me, I'll do it
because I've done it before.

00:27:02.400 --> 00:27:05.010
I would add to like if
the candidate express

00:27:05.010 --> 00:27:06.900
any interest in
continuing education

00:27:06.900 --> 00:27:08.800
programs at the company.

00:27:08.800 --> 00:27:12.640
Right so Boeing offers a lot of
continuing education programs.

00:27:12.640 --> 00:27:17.220
And so if you're like really
interested in learning

00:27:17.220 --> 00:27:19.320
and expanding one
area, say like, oh, I'm

00:27:19.320 --> 00:27:21.580
really interested in
geospatial data science, right?

00:27:21.580 --> 00:27:24.160
Because there's not a lot
of opportunities for that.

00:27:24.160 --> 00:27:25.930
Maybe when you went
to school or whatnot.

00:27:25.930 --> 00:27:28.790
And maybe that was one of the
small things on the resume.

00:27:28.790 --> 00:27:32.160
And you're like, hey, do you
offer any internal programs

00:27:32.160 --> 00:27:35.430
towards this or somewhere
where I could learn and expand

00:27:35.430 --> 00:27:36.560
that particular skill?

00:27:36.560 --> 00:27:38.760
I always look for those kind of
opportunities in an interview

00:27:38.760 --> 00:27:39.260
to.

00:27:42.690 --> 00:27:44.970
I'd love to hear
from other panelists.

00:27:44.970 --> 00:27:48.240
I think my challenge
is doing this

00:27:48.240 --> 00:27:51.330
for more junior candidates,
where often there isn't

00:27:51.330 --> 00:27:54.270
that experience, so you
kind of have to go off

00:27:54.270 --> 00:27:56.640
of how they answer things.

00:27:56.640 --> 00:27:58.110
If anybody's found
a magic bullet,

00:27:58.110 --> 00:27:59.152
I'd love to hear from it.

00:27:59.152 --> 00:28:01.920
But I think that can be the
most challenging spot for me

00:28:01.920 --> 00:28:03.560
is when somebody is junior.

00:28:03.560 --> 00:28:05.310
You don't necessarily
have the expectation

00:28:05.310 --> 00:28:07.020
that they would have had that
experience because you're

00:28:07.020 --> 00:28:08.440
hiring a junior role.

00:28:08.440 --> 00:28:10.692
To me, those are the
biggest blind spots

00:28:10.692 --> 00:28:12.900
other than kind of how they
approach the project, how

00:28:12.900 --> 00:28:14.340
they answer questions.

00:28:14.340 --> 00:28:15.750
You have kind of
the least amount

00:28:15.750 --> 00:28:18.480
to go off of to know,
OK, I know this person is

00:28:18.480 --> 00:28:20.460
going to need a lot of
support when they start.

00:28:20.460 --> 00:28:22.890
But how do I get more
reassurance that six months

00:28:22.890 --> 00:28:25.530
from their starting months
from their starting point,

00:28:25.530 --> 00:28:26.850
they're going to be one
of those people who's

00:28:26.850 --> 00:28:28.120
just kind of self-sufficient.

00:28:28.120 --> 00:28:29.860
So kind of an ass comes in.

00:28:29.860 --> 00:28:30.960
Maybe it's more vague.

00:28:30.960 --> 00:28:34.020
They will develop that
kind of more harder

00:28:34.020 --> 00:28:38.730
to find a skill set of managing
and kind of doing things

00:28:38.730 --> 00:28:40.785
from start to
finish on their own.

00:28:40.785 --> 00:28:42.660
I think that can be a
little more challenging

00:28:42.660 --> 00:28:43.535
for the junior folks.

00:28:46.650 --> 00:28:48.300
Oh, go ahead, yes, please.

00:28:48.300 --> 00:28:50.163
I just had a real
quick thing I saw

00:28:50.163 --> 00:28:52.080
someone do during an
interview that stood out,

00:28:52.080 --> 00:28:55.320
which is when you talk
about concepts or techniques

00:28:55.320 --> 00:28:57.840
that the person is not
familiar with, people

00:28:57.840 --> 00:29:00.120
with that sort of growth
mindset will make a note

00:29:00.120 --> 00:29:01.620
and engage with
you more and say,

00:29:01.620 --> 00:29:03.900
I want to learn more
about this later.

00:29:03.900 --> 00:29:06.233
Some other folks, you know,
they get really embarrassed.

00:29:06.233 --> 00:29:08.858
I don't know that, and they try
to steer the conversation away.

00:29:08.858 --> 00:29:11.490
And I thought it was impressive
when someone both admits to me,

00:29:11.490 --> 00:29:14.100
I hadn't heard of that, and
I will go, look it up later.

00:29:14.100 --> 00:29:16.058
And then sometimes you
get the follow up email,

00:29:16.058 --> 00:29:18.300
hey, I read about this like
so that really stood out

00:29:18.300 --> 00:29:20.160
to me as a positive
behavior that I think

00:29:20.160 --> 00:29:22.590
indicated the potential
for growth in the future

00:29:22.590 --> 00:29:24.240
and the interest as well.

00:29:24.240 --> 00:29:26.190
I'll throw it to you.

00:29:26.190 --> 00:29:27.690
Sure Thanks.

00:29:27.690 --> 00:29:29.310
A couple of things.

00:29:29.310 --> 00:29:32.760
I think everyone who applies
to be a data scientist at least

00:29:32.760 --> 00:29:37.220
has one thing they're
more comfortable with.

00:29:37.220 --> 00:29:40.280
You know, so whether
it's against statistics

00:29:40.280 --> 00:29:43.710
or programming in one
language versus another.

00:29:43.710 --> 00:29:46.070
You know, I ask them
about that and how

00:29:46.070 --> 00:29:50.570
they came about to
pick up those skills.

00:29:50.570 --> 00:29:53.480
It's also you can see
it also on their resumes

00:29:53.480 --> 00:29:56.180
and in their conversations
to, you know, like,

00:29:56.180 --> 00:29:59.810
hey, I stumbled upon
this because, you know,

00:29:59.810 --> 00:30:02.360
I was a graduate
student in this field

00:30:02.360 --> 00:30:04.670
and I started
doing data analysis

00:30:04.670 --> 00:30:06.500
and I realized this
is what I want to do.

00:30:06.500 --> 00:30:08.688
Not, you know, x engineering.

00:30:08.688 --> 00:30:10.730
I don't want to pick on
a particular engineering,

00:30:10.730 --> 00:30:14.660
but you know, you
know, you know,

00:30:14.660 --> 00:30:18.320
somebody in x engineering field
does a lot of data analysis

00:30:18.320 --> 00:30:21.270
and realize their
passion is in data.

00:30:21.270 --> 00:30:23.630
So tell me what specifically
about it, you know,

00:30:23.630 --> 00:30:25.760
do you like the
programming part?

00:30:25.760 --> 00:30:29.330
Everyone has a
thing that they're

00:30:29.330 --> 00:30:32.290
pretty good at better
than the other,

00:30:32.290 --> 00:30:34.430
the other aspects
of data science

00:30:34.430 --> 00:30:38.270
and/or they're much more junior.

00:30:38.270 --> 00:30:40.610
I ask about, you
know, their interests

00:30:40.610 --> 00:30:42.160
outside of data science.

00:30:42.160 --> 00:30:44.420
What's what's the
thing that you love

00:30:44.420 --> 00:30:47.810
doing ballet dancer cooking?

00:30:47.810 --> 00:30:49.310
You know, there's
always disciplines

00:30:49.310 --> 00:30:51.800
that transfer really
well, because in my mind,

00:30:51.800 --> 00:30:53.900
it's about process and practice.

00:30:53.900 --> 00:30:56.190
I don't I don't see data
science as any different.

00:30:56.190 --> 00:30:59.750
It's process and practice.

00:30:59.750 --> 00:31:04.640
I will just add kind of
quickly from my experience

00:31:04.640 --> 00:31:06.300
as being an adjunct professor.

00:31:06.300 --> 00:31:10.490
And it is interesting moving
from like this graduate frame

00:31:10.490 --> 00:31:12.920
of mind to the fact
that, you know,

00:31:12.920 --> 00:31:15.980
certain universities are now
offering undergraduate degrees

00:31:15.980 --> 00:31:17.220
in data analytics.

00:31:17.220 --> 00:31:20.707
And the breadth and
experience is a lot smaller.

00:31:20.707 --> 00:31:22.290
It's a lot smaller
of a window, right?

00:31:22.290 --> 00:31:25.580
Teaching a senior capstone
class is really giving me

00:31:25.580 --> 00:31:29.408
insight on how students
actually think about these

00:31:29.408 --> 00:31:30.950
and then thinking
about how does that

00:31:30.950 --> 00:31:33.420
translate when they go and
try to interview for a job?

00:31:33.420 --> 00:31:36.170
Because, you know, obviously,
like most of them, probably

00:31:36.170 --> 00:31:38.870
just went through the general
education requirements and then

00:31:38.870 --> 00:31:42.080
the last two or three
semesters of school,

00:31:42.080 --> 00:31:44.450
they were really cramming
in like all these kind

00:31:44.450 --> 00:31:46.820
of analytics classes,
they don't know

00:31:46.820 --> 00:31:50.300
how to describe a logistic
regression to you.

00:31:50.300 --> 00:31:52.850
You know, they're
like, they're just,

00:31:52.850 --> 00:31:56.250
you know, really starting at
that very fundamental phrase.

00:31:56.250 --> 00:31:59.690
And I will say,
like, I enjoy also

00:31:59.690 --> 00:32:02.300
mentoring the students
because in the capstone class,

00:32:02.300 --> 00:32:03.890
we spend some time
talking about how

00:32:03.890 --> 00:32:08.130
do you do interviewing strategy,
especially like on your resume.

00:32:08.130 --> 00:32:11.570
You were, you know, a like
you worked at the deli,

00:32:11.570 --> 00:32:13.250
at Safeway or something.

00:32:13.250 --> 00:32:16.035
Right and so I talk
about really expand

00:32:16.035 --> 00:32:18.410
on that experience working at
the deli counter, you know,

00:32:18.410 --> 00:32:20.280
talk about were
employee in the month.

00:32:20.280 --> 00:32:22.080
Why were you employee
of the month, right?

00:32:22.080 --> 00:32:24.860
Really accentuate
those skills and talk

00:32:24.860 --> 00:32:27.830
about your areas and
passion of why you picked

00:32:27.830 --> 00:32:29.220
this major to begin with?

00:32:29.220 --> 00:32:31.940
And then how can you
apply those to this job

00:32:31.940 --> 00:32:34.680
if you're given the
opportunity to do that?

00:32:34.680 --> 00:32:36.590
And that's usually
just general advice

00:32:36.590 --> 00:32:38.810
they give the students
because, yeah,

00:32:38.810 --> 00:32:43.730
it has been enlightening
seeing it from both ends.

00:32:43.730 --> 00:32:45.390
So, yeah, absolutely.

00:32:45.390 --> 00:32:47.390
And I think something
that everyone's touched on

00:32:47.390 --> 00:32:50.840
is this idea of being able to
articulate what skills you have

00:32:50.840 --> 00:32:52.770
and how they translate
to data science.

00:32:52.770 --> 00:32:56.090
So not all of your skills
have to be in data science,

00:32:56.090 --> 00:33:00.290
but they do still relate to
your ability to do data science.

00:33:00.290 --> 00:33:02.090
And related to that,
we have a question

00:33:02.090 --> 00:33:05.810
about thinking about
candidates and how,

00:33:05.810 --> 00:33:09.950
if at all you whathe or
non-technical contributions

00:33:09.950 --> 00:33:12.260
to the broader data
science community,

00:33:12.260 --> 00:33:16.400
whether that's through social
media, blogging, YouTube

00:33:16.400 --> 00:33:19.040
tutorials, anything
that a candidate does,

00:33:19.040 --> 00:33:21.980
that's maybe kind
of in this amorphous

00:33:21.980 --> 00:33:23.750
they're not doing
it for work, they're

00:33:23.750 --> 00:33:26.280
doing it because they're
really curious and passionate.

00:33:26.280 --> 00:33:28.940
Does that come into play
for you when analyzed

00:33:28.940 --> 00:33:30.620
or kind of deciding
on a candidate?

00:33:30.620 --> 00:33:31.420
And if so, how?

00:33:35.830 --> 00:33:37.530
But for me.

00:33:37.530 --> 00:33:44.340
I mean, it's to me, their
social media interactions are,

00:33:44.340 --> 00:33:48.480
you know, things they do on
their own time, which is great.

00:33:48.480 --> 00:33:50.460
And I don't think
it has any bearings

00:33:50.460 --> 00:33:53.870
on theatrical applications, so.

00:33:53.870 --> 00:33:56.125
So, you know, do I think
they can do the job or not?

00:33:59.760 --> 00:34:04.740
I'd say I have a slightly
different perspective.

00:34:04.740 --> 00:34:07.050
I'd say at the end
of the day, my focus

00:34:07.050 --> 00:34:09.060
is definitely can they
do the job or not?

00:34:09.060 --> 00:34:10.957
So it's not a
requirement by any means.

00:34:10.957 --> 00:34:12.540
I feel like I've
hired great folks who

00:34:12.540 --> 00:34:17.159
don't have some sort of blog
or data science side hustle.

00:34:17.159 --> 00:34:19.920
But it is a plus when I
see that because to me,

00:34:19.920 --> 00:34:24.420
I think what I do care about
is that you are depending

00:34:24.420 --> 00:34:27.360
on the level, if you're a more
senior candidate that you are

00:34:27.360 --> 00:34:31.199
going to be staying on top of
what is new and new packages

00:34:31.199 --> 00:34:34.989
and new trends, new
programs, new methods, right?

00:34:34.989 --> 00:34:38.250
Like if I'm hiring you for
doing NLP analysis like

00:34:38.250 --> 00:34:40.020
that, space is
changing so constantly,

00:34:40.020 --> 00:34:44.043
it would be impossible to think
that one person could keep up

00:34:44.043 --> 00:34:45.960
with that and all the
different things, right?

00:34:45.960 --> 00:34:48.168
So if that's what you're
going to be a specialist in.

00:34:48.168 --> 00:34:51.719
I think it's a great plus to
show your hunger for that.

00:34:51.719 --> 00:34:53.610
But I think I've
definitely seen folks

00:34:53.610 --> 00:34:56.100
who demonstrate that
hunger and that kind of up

00:34:56.100 --> 00:34:58.440
to speed with current state
who maybe don't have a blog

00:34:58.440 --> 00:35:04.565
or don't run a chapter of pi
Ladies or Ladies or something.

00:35:04.565 --> 00:35:05.940
So I think it's
a big plus, but I

00:35:05.940 --> 00:35:08.910
think you can demonstrate
it in other ways.

00:35:08.910 --> 00:35:11.420
So that's how I view it.

00:35:11.420 --> 00:35:13.340
And I'll just add,
like, you know,

00:35:13.340 --> 00:35:15.140
at Boeing, we're not
necessarily allowed

00:35:15.140 --> 00:35:18.830
to participate in certain
things and say, I always just

00:35:18.830 --> 00:35:20.902
want to add like
other hiring people.

00:35:20.902 --> 00:35:23.360
Sometimes that should weigh in
as part of the consideration

00:35:23.360 --> 00:35:23.990
as well.

00:35:23.990 --> 00:35:27.170
But with that being
said, especially like

00:35:27.170 --> 00:35:29.960
if you're working in a
research and technology branch,

00:35:29.960 --> 00:35:32.270
I would expect
you to participate

00:35:32.270 --> 00:35:35.200
in professional associations.

00:35:35.200 --> 00:35:38.540
Right especially like as part
of the technical fellowship,

00:35:38.540 --> 00:35:42.350
some of it is like your
external connections

00:35:42.350 --> 00:35:44.600
and how people are looking
at you technically,

00:35:44.600 --> 00:35:50.060
but that is like at a level of
technical excellence, right?

00:35:50.060 --> 00:35:53.000
Versus I just like
to write and blog.

00:35:53.000 --> 00:35:55.220
So I would say, like
during our interview,

